    {
      "id": "84e88eec-bdd7-518d-aa3a-69adee6dc7d2",
      "block_id": "1f292d4a-41a4-4977-9684-7c8d560b9f91",
      "input_default": {
        "model": "claude-sonnet-4-5-20250929",
        "retry": 3,
        "prompt": "You are mapping and writing a lifecycle email sequence.\n\nProduct: {{product}}\nAudience: {{audience}}\nSequence type: {{sequence_type}}\nVoice and constraints: {{voice}}\n\nWork in two passes and show both.\n\n## Pass one \u2014 the map\n\nA table with one row per email: the moment it attaches to, the trigger that\nfires it, the single goal, the subject line, and the one action you want.\n\nAn email only earns a row if it attaches to something the reader did or failed\nto do. If a moment has no detectable trigger, leave it out and say so under the\ntable rather than filling the gap with a timed send.\n\nAnchor timing to behaviour, not to a fixed calendar. Never schedule more than\none automated email in any 48 hours. State explicitly which queued emails each\nbehavioural send cancels: only the ones that action makes redundant, never the\nwhole sequence.\n\n## Pass two \u2014 the drafts\n\nWrite every email in the map. For each one:\n\n- One goal, one link to it, and that link appears twice at most.\n- A subject line that describes what is inside. No curiosity gaps.\n- Plain text unless the content is genuinely visual.\n- Short paragraphs. No preamble before the point.\n- An exit: replying, or doing the thing being asked, ends the sequence.\n\nMatch the voice and constraints given above. If they are empty, write in clear\ndirect prose with the marketing jargon stripped out.\n\nNever invent product behaviour, customer numbers, testimonials or results. Where\na draft needs a fact you have not been given, leave a marked gap in square\nbrackets and list what is missing at the end.",
        "sys_prompt": "You are a lifecycle email specialist. You write plainly, you never invent customer data, and you always leave the recipient an easy way out.",
        "prompt_values": {}
      },
